{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"codex_apps","tool":"github_search_issues","arguments":{"repo":"vamseeachanta/workspace-hub","query":"is:issue is:open label:\"status:plan-approved\" created:2026-04-01..2026-04-20","topn":100,"sort":"created","order":"desc"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"issues\": [{\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403\", \"title\": \"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike \\u2014 BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large\", \"issue_number\": 2403, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSplit from #2393 (v1 closed after MAJOR\\u00d72 cross-provider review flagged scope creep: model-selection spike bundled with implementation). This issue isolates the spike.\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\nBefore building the embeddings index, we need an evidence-based model choice. Candidates differ by cost, latency, recall, and local-vs-cloud footprint.\\n\\n## Deliverable\\n\\nA single decision document at `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` comparing:\\n- **BGE-M3** (local via Ollama \\u2014 $0 compute, higher latency)\\n- **Voyage-3** (cloud, non-OpenAI \\u2014 mid cost, strong recall)\\n- **text-embedding-3-large** (cloud, OpenAI \\u2014 low cost, strong recall)\\n\\nEvaluation rubric:\\n1. Recall@10 on a synthetic eval set (\\u226550 queries with known-good doc_key targets drawn from current L2 summaries + L3 wiki pages).\\n2. $/1000 docs at current corpus scale.\\n3. p95 latency per query.\\n4. Secret-surface + vendor lock-in footprint.\\n\\nDocument concludes with a chosen model + 2-sentence rationale.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` committed with rubric + decision\\n- [ ] Eval set at `tests/fixtures/embeddings/eval-set.jsonl` with \\u226550 queries\\n- [ ] Reproducibility script at `scripts/knowledge/run_embeddings_spike.py`\\n- [ ] Cost/latency/recall numbers cited with measurement commit SHA\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n- Blocks: #2402 (embeddings index build+query, to be filed)\\n- No hard deps on other issues.\\n\\n## Scope boundaries\\n\\n- **In scope:** comparison, measurement, decision document.\\n- **Out of scope:** production index build, query CLI, cron wiring \\u2014 these live in successor issue #2402.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403\", \"display_title\": \"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike \\u2014 BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2402\", \"title\": \"feat(doc-intel): build embeddings index L2+L3 + query CLI (single authoritative tier)\", \"issue_number\": 2402, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSplit from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2393 (v1 closed after MAJOR\\u00d72 cross-provider review flagged scope creep + storage-authority ambiguity + AC-vs-test gaps).\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\nBuild the actual embeddings index using the model chosen in vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2403. This issue focuses on build + query, with a resolved single-authority storage tier.\\n\\n## Deliverable\\n\\n- Build script: `scripts/knowledge/build_embeddings_index.py` (incremental, idempotent).\\n- Query CLI: `scripts/knowledge/query_embeddings.py`.\\n- Storage: single authoritative tier per operating-model \\u00a77 \\u2014 **decided in plan**, not left ambiguous.\\n- Manifest (git-tracked): `data/document-index/embeddings-manifest.yaml`.\\n- `sha256:`-only `doc_key` enforcement at build and query time (per operating-model \\u00a73).\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] All doc_keys in the index validated as `sha256:<hex>` per \\u00a73 \\u2014 enforced and tested\\n- [ ] Single authoritative storage tier declared in plan (repo-local OR shared-mount, not both)\\n- [ ] p95 query latency < 2s \\u2014 measured via `tests/knowledge/test_query_latency.py` (not manual)\\n- [ ] Row-count + model-pin + refresh-timestamp in `embeddings-manifest.yaml`\\n- [ ] Cost-cap guardrail has a concrete implementing file + test\\n- [ ] Nightly cron wired in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml`\\n- [ ] Threat model section: cloud-API secret sourcing, mount-path validation, cache-poisoning protection\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n- **Hard depends on: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2403** (model choice must be made first)\\n- Depends on: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#23 (`doc_key` in wiki frontmatter), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2361, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2362\\n- Blocks: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2394 (retrieval-augmented planner), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2400 (MCP wiki_search tool)\\n\\n## Scope boundaries\\n\\n- **In scope:** build, query, manifest, cost-cap, cron, secrets, threat model.\\n- **Out of scope:** downstream consumers (planner, MCP server) \\u2014 live in their own issues.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2402\", \"display_title\": \"feat(doc-intel): build embeddings index L2+L3 + query CLI (single authoritative tier)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327\", \"title\": \"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation\", \"issue_number\": 2327, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nCadQuery is a Python library for parametric 3D CAD on the OpenCASCADE B-rep kernel. STEP export is high-fidelity, so downstream meshers (gmsh, Salome) can control density without being trapped by authoring-time facets \\u2014 a good shape for offshore component geometry that feeds AQWA.\\n\\nDiscovered via HN discussion 2026-04-17 (180 points, 47 comments): https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\\n\\n> **Revised 2026-04-17 per [adversarial review](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327#issuecomment-4269065194)**: time-boxed; scope narrowed to one family and one pipeline; go/no-go criteria quantified; LLM-codegen question dropped (covered by #2328); current-state baseline required in plan.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n**Time-box:** 4 hours of hands-on work. Stop and report at the cap regardless of completeness.\\n\\n**Target pipeline (committed):** STEP (CadQuery) \\u2192 gmsh \\u2192 AQWA `QPPL DIFF` panel diffraction. Chosen because panel diffraction has the tightest mesh-quality requirements (hardest pipeline test); digitalmodel already consumes AQWA downstream; OrcaFlex line-element path is a trivial follow-up if this works.\\n\\n**Target family (committed):** H-link mooring shackle, 4 variants (MBL 45 t, 65 t, 85 t, 125 t). Chosen because mooring hardware is a recurring workspace-hub domain (#2070 cost-tracking, mooring-failures knowledge seed) and 4 variants exercises the \\\"generate N from parameters\\\" claim without inflating scope.\\n\\n## Current State (document in the plan's Resource Intel section)\\n\\n- Current `digitalmodel/` geometry pipeline \\u2014 tools, inputs, outputs\\n- Where STEP files come from today (if at all)\\n- Which solver consumers exist and their geometry-input formats\\n\\n## Go / No-Go Acceptance Criteria\\n\\nAll three must hold for **go**:\\n\\n1. **Time-to-first-STEP < 30 min** for a Python-comfortable engineer working from CadQuery docs alone (no pre-existing exemplars)\\n2. **Regenerating all 4 H-link variants < 60 s** of compute total\\n3. **STEP \\u2192 gmsh \\u2192 AQWA `QPPL DIFF`** runs with no fatal mesh errors on first attempt for at least **2 of 4** variants\\n\\n## Deliverable\\n\\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/h-link-family/` \\u2014 1 parametric script, 4 STEP outputs, 4 meshes, 1 successful AQWA run log\\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/README.md` \\u2014 timings, mesh quality notes, explicit verdict against the 3 criteria above\\n\\n## Planning Workflow (per CLAUDE.md)\\n\\nPlan file: `docs/plans/2026-MM-DD-issue-2327-cadquery-digitalmodel-spike.md` using `docs/plans/_template-issue-plan.md`. Resource-intel section must include the current-state documentation above. No implementation until `status:plan-approved`.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Wiki entity: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\\n- Source summary: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`\\n- Marine cross-page: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\\n- Sibling issues: #2328 (tool comparison \\u2014 covers the LLM-codegen question), #2329 (methodology decision \\u2014 blocked by this)\\n- HN thread: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\\n- CadQuery: https://cadquery.github.io/\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327\", \"display_title\": \"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269\", \"title\": \"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation\", \"issue_number\": 2269, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nStandardize the canonical OpenFOAM baseline for portable engineering work, using repo-tracked docs, scripts, example cases, and validation.\\n\\nPart of #1782.\\nPrimary reference: #25.\\nSupports downstream work such as #1268.\\n\\n## Motivation\\nOpenFOAM is already CLI-native, but the baseline workflow is not yet fully encoded as a portable artifact bundle. We need a declared version target, canonical entrypoints, and a smoke validation path.\\n\\n## Scope\\nCreate the baseline OpenFOAM portability package in workspace-hub.\\n\\nExpected deliverables:\\n- baseline docs\\n- run/validation entrypoints\\n- minimal smoke example\\n- troubleshooting guidance\\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] baseline doc declares the target fork/version explicitly (default: ESI/OpenFOAM.com v2312 unless changed by decision)\\n- [ ] canonical runner command(s) are documented\\n- [ ] minimal smoke case exists under a reproducible repo-tracked path\\n- [ ] validator produces pass/fail output with explicit checks\\n- [ ] common failure modes and version/fork mismatches are documented\\n- [ ] workflow can be executed on the canonical engineering host with documented prerequisites\\n\\n## Notes\\nImplementation should prefer:\\n- explicit version detection and fail-fast behavior\\n- repo-tracked wrappers over tribal command memory\\n- logs/results/validation outputs in predictable locations\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269\", \"display_title\": \"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2229\", \"title\": \"feat(windows-parity): validate licensed-win-1 NightlyReadiness and MemoryBridgeSync live\", \"issue_number\": 2229, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nNow that the repo-side Windows parity hardening is in place (tracked readiness proof path, MemoryBridgeSync scheduler task, bridge script cross-platform updates), run the live validation on `licensed-win-1` and replace the bootstrap placeholder artifact with real machine evidence.\\n\\n## Why\\nThe repo now contains a bootstrap placeholder at `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml`, but confidence still depends on a real Windows execution cycle proving that:\\n- `NightlyReadiness` writes the expected shared report\\n- `MemoryBridgeSync` runs successfully under Git Bash/Task Scheduler\\n- Windows-generated repo memory outputs refresh and push cleanly\\n- `compare-harness-state.sh` reflects real Windows status instead of bootstrap placeholder data\\n\\n## Scope\\n- On `licensed-win-1`, install/update the scheduled tasks from `scripts/windows/setup-scheduler-tasks.ps1`\\n- Run `NightlyReadiness` manually once and capture the produced readiness YAML\\n- Run `MemoryBridgeSync` manually once and verify `.claude/memory/` refresh behavior\\n- Replace the placeholder `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml` with real output\\n- Record exact commands, logs, and any Windows-specific failures\\n- Update docs only if live behavior differs from current assumptions\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- Real `licensed-win-1` readiness artifact committed to repo\\n- Evidence that `MemoryBridgeSync` succeeds or a concrete failure report\\n- Short operator note on how to re-run both tasks manually on Windows\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] `scripts/windows/setup-scheduler-tasks.ps1` registers all expected tasks on `licensed-win-1`\\n- [ ] `NightlyReadiness` produces a non-placeholder `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml`\\n- [ ] `MemoryBridgeSync` runs on Windows Git Bash and updates repo-tracked memory outputs without breaking Linux behavior\\n- [ ] Any remaining Windows-specific failures are documented with exact repro steps\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2229\", \"display_title\": \"feat(windows-parity): validate licensed-win-1 NightlyReadiness and MemoryBridgeSync live\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2152\", \"title\": \"test(reporting): add golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage\", \"issue_number\": 2152, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd the golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage.\\n\\n## Why\\nValidator and renderer work need stable example artifacts representing healthy, degraded, and invalid runs. Fixtures also flush out schema ambiguities early.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- add valid weekly-review run artifact fixtures for minimal, full, degraded, and multi-machine runs\\n- add invalid fixtures for missing keys, bad enums, bad timestamps, and malformed sections\\n- define expected validator outcomes for invalid cases\\n- document fixture naming and refresh rules\\n- wire fixtures into validator CI tests\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- valid and invalid fixture corpus\\n- expected validator outputs\\n- fixture README\\n- CI test usage\\n\\n## Parent\\n- Parent issues: #2146, #2147, #2089\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2152\", \"display_title\": \"test(reporting): add golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2124\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): extend ingestion to Orcina resources, examples, and training materials\", \"issue_number\": 2124, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nExtend llm-wiki coverage to remaining Orcina web content beyond the help system.\\n\\n## Sources not yet ingested\\n1. **Resources page** \\u2014 https://www.orcina.com/resources/ (videos, webinars, case studies)\\n2. **Examples descriptions** \\u2014 each of the 54 OrcaFlex examples has an online description\\n3. **Python training ZIP** \\u2014 https://www.orcina.com/wp-content/uploads/training/An%20introduction%20to%20the%20Python%20interface%20to%20OrcaFlex.zip\\n4. **Release notes** \\u2014 version-by-version changelog (behavioral changes, new features)\\n5. **Blog/news posts** \\u2014 https://www.orcina.com/news/ (application insights)\\n\\n## Context\\n- Parent: #2088 (core help ingested \\u2014 717 topics)\\n- Papers already done: 20 PDFs from papers-and-technical-notes page\\n- Existing script: `scripts/data/llm-wiki/ingest-orcina.py`\\n\\n## Value\\nRelease notes are especially valuable \\u2014 they document behavioral changes that affect model compatibility across versions. Training materials fill the gap between reference docs and practical usage.\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2124\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): extend ingestion to Orcina resources, examples, and training materials\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2125\", \"title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): auto-refresh ingestion on new Orcina releases\", \"issue_number\": 2125, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd version detection and incremental refresh to the llm-wiki ingestion pipeline so new OrcaFlex releases are automatically captured.\\n\\n## Current state\\n- ingest-orcina.py does a full crawl every run (~4 min for 717 pages)\\n- No version tracking or diff detection\\n- Current version: OrcaFlex 11.6c\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n1. Version detection: parse release page to detect new versions\\n2. Incremental mode: only fetch pages that changed (use HTTP Last-Modified or content hash)\\n3. Changelog generation: diff new vs old index to show what changed\\n4. Optional: schedule via cron or GSD trigger on version bump\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n- `ingest-orcina.py --refresh` only downloads changed pages\\n- Version stored in index.json metadata\\n- Diff report written to data/llm-wiki/changelog/\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2125\", \"display_title\": \"feat(llm-wiki): auto-refresh ingestion on new Orcina releases\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112\", \"title\": \"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking\", \"issue_number\": 2112, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nBackfill the minimum SubseaIQ equipment-count fields required to unblock #2055 subsea cost benchmarking.\\n\\n## Context\\nAssessment of #2055 found two active blockers:\\n1. `status:needs-data`\\n2. the required equipment-count fields are absent from every current GoM record in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n\\nCurrent state from assessment:\\n- required threshold: >=10 GoM fields with equipment counts\\n- current state: 0/10 records have `num_trees`, `num_manifolds`, or `tieback_distance_km`\\n\\nWithout this backfill, the cost benchmarking implementation issue cannot proceed honestly.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Backfill or derive, for at least 10 GoM fields, the following fields in the SubseaIQ-derived dataset:\\n  - `num_trees`\\n  - `num_manifolds`\\n  - `tieback_distance_km`\\n- Document provenance / confidence for each backfilled field\\n- Update the dataset and any ingestion/normalization helpers needed to preserve the fields\\n- Add a minimal validation check proving the threshold is satisfied\\n\\n## Target data\\n- `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n- any normalization or ingestion surfaces that write/read these fields\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- At least 10 GoM records contain non-null equipment counts and tieback distance\\n- Provenance/confidence is documented\\n- A validation command or test confirms the threshold is met\\n- #2055 can remove `status:needs-data` after verification\\n\\n## Relationship\\n- Direct prerequisite for #2055\\n- Should be completed before any cost-per-tree / cost-per-manifold implementation work\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112\", \"display_title\": \"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055\", \"title\": \"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts\", \"issue_number\": 2055, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nBuild cost-per-equipment-unit benchmark curves (cost/tree, cost/km-flowline, cost/manifold) by water-depth band, correlating SubseaIQ equipment counts against sanctioned project costs.\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\nThe scaffold (#1861) provides `SubseaProject` with equipment-count fields and `load_projects()`, but zero cost functions exist. No module currently correlates equipment counts with project costs.\\n\\n## Data Prerequisites (must be met before implementation)\\n\\n- [ ] Equipment counts (num_trees, num_manifolds, tieback_distance_km) backfilled for >= 10 GoM fields in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n- [ ] Confirmed name-match mapping between SubseaIQ project names and CostDataPoint project names for overlapping records\\n\\n## v1 Scope (band-level aggregation)\\n\\nUse depth-band-level aggregation to compute cost-per-equipment ratios. This avoids per-project name-match fragility and works with sparse data.\\n\\n### New functions in `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/field_development/benchmarks.py`:\\n- `cost_per_tree(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `cost_per_km_flowline(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `cost_per_manifold(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `unit_cost_curves(projects) -> dict` combining all three metrics\\n- `cost_benchmark_bands(projects) -> dict` with low/base/high by depth band and concept type\\n\\n### New helper in `worldenergydata/subseaiq/analytics/cost_correlation.py`:\\n- `correlate_equipment_costs(subseaiq_records, cost_records)` \\u2014 depth-band-level join\\n- `aggregate_costs_by_depth_band(merged)` \\u2014 band-level cost aggregation\\n\\n## Deferred to v2\\n\\n- Per-project cost-per-tree correlation (requires CostDataPoint schema extension with equipment fields)\\n- Cross-validation of equipment-derived costs against individual sanctioned project costs\\n- Full 71-record equipment-count backfill\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Equipment counts populated for >= 10 GoM fields\\n- [ ] `cost_per_tree` returns values in $10M-$200M range per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_per_km_flowline` returns values in $5M-$50M range per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_per_manifold` returns physically reasonable values per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_benchmark_bands` returns nested dict with low/base/high structure\\n- [ ] All 27 existing `test_benchmarks.py` tests pass unchanged\\n- [ ] New cost-related tests added and passing\\n- [ ] `benchmarks` exported from `digitalmodel.field_development.__init__`\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n- #1861 (scaffold) \\u2014 DONE\\n- Equipment-count data backfill \\u2014 REQUIRED before implementation\\n\\n## Labels\\n\\n`enhancement`, `priority:high`, `cat:engineering`, `dark-intelligence`, `agent:claude`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055\", \"display_title\": \"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2046\", \"title\": \"Audit compliance of strict issue planning workflow after rollout\", \"issue_number\": 2046, \"body\": \"After the new strict planning workflow has been used for a short period, audit compliance across agent activity.\\n\\nAudit focus:\\n- Was `issue-planning-mode` used for all issues?\\n- Were plans created in `docs/plans/` using the template?\\n- Were adversarial reviews completed before user review?\\n- Were labels `status:plan-review` and `status:plan-approved` used correctly?\\n- Did any agent begin coding before approval?\\n\\nDeliverables:\\n- Markdown report under `docs/reports/`\\n- Compliance summary with examples\\n- Gaps, failure modes, and recommendations\\n- Decision: keep current approach or escalate enforcement\\n\\nSuggested trigger:\\n- Run after 1-2 weeks of usage or after at least 10 issues have gone through the new workflow\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2046\", \"display_title\": \"Audit compliance of strict issue planning workflow after rollout\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962\", \"title\": \"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring \\u2014 audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode\", \"issue_number\": 1962, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nSystematic refactoring of the workspace-hub repo ecosystem, starting with tier-1 repositories. The goal is to reduce architectural debt, consolidate duplicate patterns, enforce consistent structure, and improve agent navigability across all tier-1 repos.\\n\\n## Tier-1 Repos (Priority Order)\\n\\n| # | Repo | Purpose | Maturity | Depends On |\\n|---|------|---------|----------|------------|\\n| 1 | `digitalmodel/` | Engineering digital twin (asset integrity, CP, GIS, hydro) | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 2 | `assetutilities/` | Shared engineering utilities (config, units, calculations) | stable | \\u2014 |\\n| 3 | `assethold/` | Asset portfolio financial analysis | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 4 | `worldenergydata/` | Global energy market data (BSEE, EIA, drilling) | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 5 | `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS/` | CAD solvers, models, database, utilities | beta | \\u2014 |\\n| 6 | `aceengineer-website/` | Consulting website | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| 7 | `aceengineer-strategy/` | GTM, business strategy | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| 8 | `sabithaandkrishnaestates/` | SKEstates Inc. business ops | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n\\n## Refactoring Scope\\n\\n### Phase 1: Audit (analysis-only, no code changes)\\n- [ ] Run `repo-architecture-analysis` on each tier-1 repo\\n- [ ] Identify cross-repo duplicate code (especially assetutilities consumers)\\n- [ ] Map dependency graph (imports, shared configs, data flows)\\n- [ ] Catalog test coverage per repo (current state)\\n- [ ] Identify orphaned/dead code, unused modules, oversized files (>400 lines)\\n- [ ] Score each repo against the Control-Plane Contract (AGENTS.md, provider adapters)\\n- [ ] Identify skill duplicates across repos (134 known from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 audit)\\n\\n### Phase 2: Plan (per-repo refactor plans)\\n- [ ] Generate refactor plan per tier-1 repo with estimated effort\\n- [ ] Prioritize by: business value \\u00d7 technical debt severity \\u00d7 dependency impact\\n- [ ] Identify breaking changes and migration paths\\n- [ ] Define test coverage targets (tier-1 = 85% per pytest.tier1.ini)\\n\\n### Phase 3: Execute (TDD, incremental, per-repo)\\n- [ ] Execute refactors repo-by-repo following dependency order (assetutilities first)\\n- [ ] TDD mandatory \\u2014 tests before implementation\\n- [ ] Each repo refactor as a separate sub-issue for tracking\\n\\n## Execution Strategy: Claude Code Plan Mode (Cloud)\\n\\n### Claude Code `--permission-mode plan` (\\\"Ultraplan\\\")\\n\\nClaude Code v2.1.92 supports `--permission-mode plan` which restricts the agent to **read-only analysis and planning** \\u2014 no file writes, no shell mutations. This is ideal for Phase 1 (Audit) and Phase 2 (Plan).\\n\\n**Proposed execution:**\\n\\n```bash\\n# Phase 1: Audit each tier-1 repo in plan mode (safe, read-only)\\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\\\\n  --print \\\\\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\\\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\\\\n  -p \\\"Audit the repository architecture of digitalmodel/. Analyze: module structure, dependency graph, test coverage, dead code, file sizes >400 lines, AGENTS.md compliance. Output a structured JSON report.\\\"\\n\\n# Phase 2: Generate refactor plan\\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\\\\n  --print \\\\\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\\\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\\\\n  -p \\\"Given the audit report, generate a prioritized refactor plan for digitalmodel/. Include: breaking changes, migration steps, test targets, estimated effort per module.\\\"\\n```\\n\\n### Cloud Execution Considerations\\n\\n| Aspect | Status | Notes |\\n|--------|--------|-------|\\n| **Claude Code CLI** | Installed (v2.1.92) | Available on ace-linux-1 |\\n| **Plan mode** | Supported | `--permission-mode plan` \\u2014 read-only, safe for audit |\\n| **API-key mode** | Supported | `--bare` + `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for headless/cloud runs |\\n| **Print mode** | Supported | `-p` / `--print` for non-interactive batch execution |\\n| **Budget cap** | Supported | `--max-budget-usd` to control spend per run |\\n| **Cloud sandbox** | Possible | Can run via Hermes `delegate_task` with `acp_command='claude'` |\\n| **Max subscription** | Claude Max $200/mo | Rate-limited but sufficient for plan-mode audit |\\n\\n### Recommended Approach\\n1. **Phase 1 + 2 (Audit + Plan)**: Run Claude Code in plan mode via `delegate_task(acp_command='claude')` \\u2014 subagents get isolated context, read-only, budget-capped\\n2. **Phase 3 (Execute)**: Requires interactive Claude Code sessions with full permissions \\u2014 NOT suited for autonomous cloud execution without user oversight\\n\\n## Adversarial Review Requirements\\n\\nPer [AI Review Routing Policy](docs/standards/AI_REVIEW_ROUTING_POLICY.md):\\n\\n- **3-agent adversarial review at BOTH stages** (plan + code/artifact)\\n- Claude: orchestrator + reviewer\\n- Codex: implementation worker + adversarial reviewer\\n- Gemini: architecture reviewer + adversarial reviewer\\n- Max 3 review iterations per deliverable\\n- All MAJOR findings must be resolved before completion\\n\\n### Review Checkpoints\\n1. **After Phase 1 (Audit)**: All 3 agents review the audit findings\\n2. **After Phase 2 (Plan)**: All 3 agents review each repo's refactor plan\\n3. **After Phase 3 (Execute)**: All 3 agents review code changes per repo\\n\\n## Related Issues\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1567 \\u2014 Feature: Continuous Repo Architecture Intelligence\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1545 \\u2014 Agentic feature progression for repo ecosystem productivity under GSD\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1603 \\u2014 Multi-repo architecture scan \\u2014 expand scanner to all tier-1 repos\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 \\u2014 Cross-agent session corpus audit (134 duplicate skills identified)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1734 \\u2014 chore: add domain skills to assethold (tier-1 with 0 skills)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1661 \\u2014 Architecture scanner: dependency cycle detection and layering violations\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#113 \\u2014 ROADMAP: Repo ecosystem 3-6 month horizon\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] Audit report generated for each tier-1 repo\\n- [ ] Refactor plan per repo reviewed and approved by all 3 agents\\n- [ ] Phase 3 sub-issues created with clear scope per repo\\n- [ ] Test coverage meets tier-1 target (85%) after refactoring\\n- [ ] Zero cross-repo duplicate skills remaining\\n- [ ] All repos score 100% on Control-Plane Contract compliance\\n- [ ] Dependency graph documented and validated\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962\", \"display_title\": \"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring \\u2014 audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1782\", \"title\": \"epic: zero-loss agent learnings \\u2014 git-track ALL AI agent memories, corrections, patterns, and insights\", \"issue_number\": 1782, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nA full audit of all 4 AI agents (Claude Code, Hermes, Codex, Gemini) revealed that **~6.7GB of machine-local state** has NO git protection. If ace-linux-1 dies, the entire institutional memory built over months of agent interaction is lost.\\n\\nThe learnings that matter most \\u2014 user corrections, learned patterns, permission rules, cross-agent insights \\u2014 total only ~400KB but are scattered across 4 different home directories with no unified backup or portability strategy.\\n\\nThis is the umbrella issue coordinating the fix across all agents.\\n\\n## Architecture: Three Tiers of Portability\\n\\n### Tier 1: Git-committed snapshots (survives `git clone`)\\nSmall, high-value files snapshotted nightly into workspace-hub repo:\\n- Agent memory files (Hermes MEMORY.md/USER.md, Claude project memory, Codex rules)\\n- Distilled learning artifacts (patterns, corrections, insights, drift summaries)\\n- Exported session metadata (JSONL tool-call summaries, not full transcripts)\\n\\n### Tier 2: Rsync backup (survives single-machine failure)\\nLarge files backed up to ace-linux-2 daily:\\n- Full session transcripts (Claude 2.5GB, Codex 899MB, Gemini 162MB, Hermes 124MB)\\n- SQLite databases (Hermes state.db 53MB, Codex state_5.sqlite 59MB)\\n\\n### Tier 3: Regenerable (no backup needed)\\nCaches, plugins, debug logs, telemetry \\u2014 recreated on agent install.\\n\\n## Child Issues\\n\\n| # | Issue | Agent | What's tracked | Size | Status |\\n|---|-------|-------|---------------|------|--------|\\n| #1777 | Hermes memory snapshots | Hermes | MEMORY.md, USER.md | 3KB | NEW |\\n| #1778 | Un-gitignore hermes/cross-agent state | Hermes/Cross | hermes-insights.yaml, cross-agent-memory.yaml, session JSONL | 15KB+11MB | NEW |\\n| #1779 | Claude project memory snapshots | Claude | 39 feedback/project/reference memory files | 196KB | NEW |\\n| #1780 | Fix nightly pipeline git-add gap | Claude/All | corrections/, patterns/, cc-insights/, reflect-history/, learned-patterns.json, etc. | ~25MB | NEW |\\n| #1781 | Codex + Gemini state snapshots | Codex/Gemini | default.rules, history.jsonl, state.json, session export | ~350KB | NEW |\\n| #1583 | Hermes config parity | Hermes | config.yaml template, SOUL.md | existing | OPEN |\\n| #894 | Knowledge persistence architecture | All | WRK completion learnings, career knowledge | existing | OPEN |\\n| #194 | Codex session archive integration | Codex | Session JSONL export pipeline | existing | OPEN |\\n| #193 | Session scanner for historical learnings | Claude | Retroactive learning extraction | existing | OPEN |\\n\\n## Implementation Order\\n\\n1. **#1780** (fix nightly pipeline) \\u2014 Lowest effort, highest impact. Just add `git add` + commit for files already allowed by .gitignore exceptions. ~25MB of corrections, patterns, insights immediately saved.\\n\\n2. **#1778** (un-gitignore hermes state) \\u2014 Add 7 gitignore exceptions + commit existing files. Hermes insights, cross-agent memory, drift summary, portfolio signals.\\n\\n3. **#1779** (Claude project memory) \\u2014 Snapshot 39 memory files (196KB). Most valuable per-byte: every `feedback_*.md` is a user correction.\\n\\n4. **#1777** (Hermes memory) \\u2014 Snapshot MEMORY.md + USER.md (3KB).\\n\\n5. **#1781** (Codex + Gemini) \\u2014 Snapshot rules, state, history. Build Codex session export pipeline.\\n\\n6. **#1583** (config parity) \\u2014 Already scoped, extends to ace-linux-2.\\n\\n## Nightly Pipeline Changes (consolidated)\\n\\nAll snapshot + commit logic should be consolidated into ONE script called at the end of `comprehensive-learning-nightly.sh`:\\n\\n```bash\\nscripts/cron/commit-learning-artifacts.sh\\n```\\n\\nThis script:\\n1. Snapshots agent memories (Hermes, Claude, Codex, Gemini) to `config/agents/*/`\\n2. `git add` all learning state directories\\n3. Runs `legal-sanity-scan.sh --diff-only` (MANDATORY before commit)\\n4. Commits and pushes if anything changed\\n\\n## Legal Risk\\n\\nMemory files and correction records can contain file paths, command strings, or project names that reference client work. ALL snapshot directories must be covered by `legal-sanity-scan.sh`. The commit script must fail-safe: if legal scan finds violations, skip the commit and log the issue.\\n\\n## What a Fresh `git clone` Gets You After This\\n\\n```\\nconfig/agents/\\n  hermes/memories/          # Hermes MEMORY.md + USER.md snapshots\\n  claude/memory-snapshots/  # 39 Claude project memory files\\n  codex/state-snapshots/    # default.rules + history.jsonl\\n  gemini/state-snapshots/   # state.json + projects.json\\n\\n.claude/state/\\n  corrections/              # Error correction history\\n  patterns/                 # Learned behavioral patterns\\n  reflect-history/          # Self-reflection analysis\\n  cc-insights/              # Agent insights\\n  learned-patterns.json     # Distilled pattern database\\n  cc-user-insights.yaml     # User preference model\\n  hermes-insights.yaml      # Cross-agent Hermes export\\n  cross-agent-memory.yaml   # Unified memory index\\n  drift-summary.yaml        # Config/skill drift tracking\\n  portfolio-signals.yaml    # Repo health signals\\n  skill-eval-results/       # Skill evaluation scores\\n\\nlogs/orchestrator/hermes/   # Hermes session exports (JSONL)\\nlogs/orchestrator/codex/    # Codex session exports (JSONL, future)\\n```\\n\\nTotal: ~40MB of institutional memory that survives machine loss.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] All child issues closed\\n- [ ] `git clone` on a fresh machine + `sync-agent-configs.sh` restores all agent memories\\n- [ ] Nightly pipeline commits all learning artifacts without manual intervention\\n- [ ] Legal scan gates all committed learning data\\n- [ ] Session transcript rsync backup covers all 4 agents\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1782\", \"display_title\": \"epic: zero-loss agent learnings \\u2014 git-track ALL AI agent memories, corrections, patterns, and insights\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583\", \"title\": \"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates\", \"issue_number\": 1583, \"body\": \"## Context\\nClaude, Codex, and Gemini agent configs are managed via repo templates in `config/agents/{claude,codex,gemini}/` and synced to home directories by `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`. Hermes is not yet covered.\\n\\nThe `harness-update.sh` cron script already has `update_hermes()` but only runs on ace-linux-1. ace-linux-2 needs inclusion.\\n\\n## What Needs to Happen\\n\\n### A) Add Hermes templates to repo\\n- `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` \\u2014 model defaults, toolsets, terminal (no secrets)\\n- `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` \\u2014 persona template\\n\\n### B) Extend sync-agent-configs.sh\\nAdd Hermes to `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`:\\n- Template: `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template`\\n- Target: `~/.hermes/config.yaml`\\n- Merge strategy: template provides defaults, machine-local overrides preserved\\n- Skip auth.json and .env (secrets, never in repo)\\n\\n### C) Custom skills versioning\\n- Bundled skills managed by `hermes update` (no action needed)\\n- Custom workflow skills \\u2192 `config/agents/hermes/custom-skills/` in repo\\n- Sync script copies/symlinks to `~/.hermes/skills/`\\n\\n### D) Add ace-linux-2 to harness-update cron\\nIn `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml`:\\n```yaml\\n# Change from:\\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1]\\n# To:\\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1, dev-secondary, ace-linux-2]\\n```\\nThen run `setup-cron.sh` on ace-linux-2.\\n\\n### E) Memories policy\\n- `MEMORY.md` and `USER.md` are personal/accumulated \\u2014 do NOT sync via repo\\n- They build naturally per machine as Hermes is used\\n- Existing `claude-memory-backup` cron already backs up to ace-linux-2\\n\\n## Files to Create/Modify\\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` (new)\\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` (new)\\n- [ ] `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh` (extend)\\n- [ ] `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` (add ace-linux-2 to harness-update)\\n- [ ] `docs/ops/scheduled-tasks.md` (update documentation)\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] Hermes config template exists in repo\\n- [ ] sync-agent-configs.sh handles Hermes merge\\n- [ ] harness-update cron runs on ace-linux-2\\n- [ ] `bash scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh --dry-run` shows Hermes sync\\n- [ ] Running sync on a new machine bootstraps Hermes config correctly\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583\", \"display_title\": \"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates\"}]}"}],"structured_content":{"issues":[{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403","title":"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike — BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large","issue_number":2403,"body":"## Context\n\nSplit from #2393 (v1 closed after MAJOR×2 cross-provider review flagged scope creep: model-selection spike bundled with implementation). This issue isolates the spike.\n\n## Problem\n\nBefore building the embeddings index, we need an evidence-based model choice. Candidates differ by cost, latency, recall, and local-vs-cloud footprint.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nA single decision document at `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` comparing:\n- **BGE-M3** (local via Ollama — $0 compute, higher latency)\n- **Voyage-3** (cloud, non-OpenAI — mid cost, strong recall)\n- **text-embedding-3-large** (cloud, OpenAI — low cost, strong recall)\n\nEvaluation rubric:\n1. Recall@10 on a synthetic eval set (≥50 queries with known-good doc_key targets drawn from current L2 summaries + L3 wiki pages).\n2. $/1000 docs at current corpus scale.\n3. p95 latency per query.\n4. Secret-surface + vendor lock-in footprint.\n\nDocument concludes with a chosen model + 2-sentence rationale.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` committed with rubric + decision\n- [ ] Eval set at `tests/fixtures/embeddings/eval-set.jsonl` with ≥50 queries\n- [ ] Reproducibility script at `scripts/knowledge/run_embeddings_spike.py`\n- [ ] Cost/latency/recall numbers cited with measurement commit SHA\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Blocks: #2402 (embeddings index build+query, to be filed)\n- No hard deps on other issues.\n\n## Scope boundaries\n\n- **In scope:** comparison, measurement, decision document.\n- **Out of scope:** production index build, query CLI, cron wiring — these live in successor issue #2402.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403","display_title":"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike — BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2402","title":"feat(doc-intel): build embeddings index L2+L3 + query CLI (single authoritative tier)","issue_number":2402,"body":"## Context\n\nSplit from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2393 (v1 closed after MAJOR×2 cross-provider review flagged scope creep + storage-authority ambiguity + AC-vs-test gaps).\n\n## Problem\n\nBuild the actual embeddings index using the model chosen in vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2403. This issue focuses on build + query, with a resolved single-authority storage tier.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- Build script: `scripts/knowledge/build_embeddings_index.py` (incremental, idempotent).\n- Query CLI: `scripts/knowledge/query_embeddings.py`.\n- Storage: single authoritative tier per operating-model §7 — **decided in plan**, not left ambiguous.\n- Manifest (git-tracked): `data/document-index/embeddings-manifest.yaml`.\n- `sha256:`-only `doc_key` enforcement at build and query time (per operating-model §3).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] All doc_keys in the index validated as `sha256:<hex>` per §3 — enforced and tested\n- [ ] Single authoritative storage tier declared in plan (repo-local OR shared-mount, not both)\n- [ ] p95 query latency < 2s — measured via `tests/knowledge/test_query_latency.py` (not manual)\n- [ ] Row-count + model-pin + refresh-timestamp in `embeddings-manifest.yaml`\n- [ ] Cost-cap guardrail has a concrete implementing file + test\n- [ ] Nightly cron wired in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml`\n- [ ] Threat model section: cloud-API secret sourcing, mount-path validation, cache-poisoning protection\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- **Hard depends on: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2403** (model choice must be made first)\n- Depends on: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#23 (`doc_key` in wiki frontmatter), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2361, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2362\n- Blocks: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2394 (retrieval-augmented planner), vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2400 (MCP wiki_search tool)\n\n## Scope boundaries\n\n- **In scope:** build, query, manifest, cost-cap, cron, secrets, threat model.\n- **Out of scope:** downstream consumers (planner, MCP server) — live in their own issues.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2402","display_title":"feat(doc-intel): build embeddings index L2+L3 + query CLI (single authoritative tier)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327","title":"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation","issue_number":2327,"body":"## Context\n\nCadQuery is a Python library for parametric 3D CAD on the OpenCASCADE B-rep kernel. STEP export is high-fidelity, so downstream meshers (gmsh, Salome) can control density without being trapped by authoring-time facets — a good shape for offshore component geometry that feeds AQWA.\n\nDiscovered via HN discussion 2026-04-17 (180 points, 47 comments): https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\n\n> **Revised 2026-04-17 per [adversarial review](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327#issuecomment-4269065194)**: time-boxed; scope narrowed to one family and one pipeline; go/no-go criteria quantified; LLM-codegen question dropped (covered by #2328); current-state baseline required in plan.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Time-box:** 4 hours of hands-on work. Stop and report at the cap regardless of completeness.\n\n**Target pipeline (committed):** STEP (CadQuery) → gmsh → AQWA `QPPL DIFF` panel diffraction. Chosen because panel diffraction has the tightest mesh-quality requirements (hardest pipeline test); digitalmodel already consumes AQWA downstream; OrcaFlex line-element path is a trivial follow-up if this works.\n\n**Target family (committed):** H-link mooring shackle, 4 variants (MBL 45 t, 65 t, 85 t, 125 t). Chosen because mooring hardware is a recurring workspace-hub domain (#2070 cost-tracking, mooring-failures knowledge seed) and 4 variants exercises the \"generate N from parameters\" claim without inflating scope.\n\n## Current State (document in the plan's Resource Intel section)\n\n- Current `digitalmodel/` geometry pipeline — tools, inputs, outputs\n- Where STEP files come from today (if at all)\n- Which solver consumers exist and their geometry-input formats\n\n## Go / No-Go Acceptance Criteria\n\nAll three must hold for **go**:\n\n1. **Time-to-first-STEP < 30 min** for a Python-comfortable engineer working from CadQuery docs alone (no pre-existing exemplars)\n2. **Regenerating all 4 H-link variants < 60 s** of compute total\n3. **STEP → gmsh → AQWA `QPPL DIFF`** runs with no fatal mesh errors on first attempt for at least **2 of 4** variants\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/h-link-family/` — 1 parametric script, 4 STEP outputs, 4 meshes, 1 successful AQWA run log\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/README.md` — timings, mesh quality notes, explicit verdict against the 3 criteria above\n\n## Planning Workflow (per CLAUDE.md)\n\nPlan file: `docs/plans/2026-MM-DD-issue-2327-cadquery-digitalmodel-spike.md` using `docs/plans/_template-issue-plan.md`. Resource-intel section must include the current-state documentation above. No implementation until `status:plan-approved`.\n\n## References\n\n- Wiki entity: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\n- Source summary: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`\n- Marine cross-page: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\n- Sibling issues: #2328 (tool comparison — covers the LLM-codegen question), #2329 (methodology decision — blocked by this)\n- HN thread: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\n- CadQuery: https://cadquery.github.io/","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327","display_title":"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269","title":"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation","issue_number":2269,"body":"## Summary\nStandardize the canonical OpenFOAM baseline for portable engineering work, using repo-tracked docs, scripts, example cases, and validation.\n\nPart of #1782.\nPrimary reference: #25.\nSupports downstream work such as #1268.\n\n## Motivation\nOpenFOAM is already CLI-native, but the baseline workflow is not yet fully encoded as a portable artifact bundle. We need a declared version target, canonical entrypoints, and a smoke validation path.\n\n## Scope\nCreate the baseline OpenFOAM portability package in workspace-hub.\n\nExpected deliverables:\n- baseline docs\n- run/validation entrypoints\n- minimal smoke example\n- troubleshooting guidance\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] baseline doc declares the target fork/version explicitly (default: ESI/OpenFOAM.com v2312 unless changed by decision)\n- [ ] canonical runner command(s) are documented\n- [ ] minimal smoke case exists under a reproducible repo-tracked path\n- [ ] validator produces pass/fail output with explicit checks\n- [ ] common failure modes and version/fork mismatches are documented\n- [ ] workflow can be executed on the canonical engineering host with documented prerequisites\n\n## Notes\nImplementation should prefer:\n- explicit version detection and fail-fast behavior\n- repo-tracked wrappers over tribal command memory\n- logs/results/validation outputs in predictable locations\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269","display_title":"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2229","title":"feat(windows-parity): validate licensed-win-1 NightlyReadiness and MemoryBridgeSync live","issue_number":2229,"body":"## Summary\nNow that the repo-side Windows parity hardening is in place (tracked readiness proof path, MemoryBridgeSync scheduler task, bridge script cross-platform updates), run the live validation on `licensed-win-1` and replace the bootstrap placeholder artifact with real machine evidence.\n\n## Why\nThe repo now contains a bootstrap placeholder at `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml`, but confidence still depends on a real Windows execution cycle proving that:\n- `NightlyReadiness` writes the expected shared report\n- `MemoryBridgeSync` runs successfully under Git Bash/Task Scheduler\n- Windows-generated repo memory outputs refresh and push cleanly\n- `compare-harness-state.sh` reflects real Windows status instead of bootstrap placeholder data\n\n## Scope\n- On `licensed-win-1`, install/update the scheduled tasks from `scripts/windows/setup-scheduler-tasks.ps1`\n- Run `NightlyReadiness` manually once and capture the produced readiness YAML\n- Run `MemoryBridgeSync` manually once and verify `.claude/memory/` refresh behavior\n- Replace the placeholder `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml` with real output\n- Record exact commands, logs, and any Windows-specific failures\n- Update docs only if live behavior differs from current assumptions\n\n## Deliverables\n- Real `licensed-win-1` readiness artifact committed to repo\n- Evidence that `MemoryBridgeSync` succeeds or a concrete failure report\n- Short operator note on how to re-run both tasks manually on Windows\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `scripts/windows/setup-scheduler-tasks.ps1` registers all expected tasks on `licensed-win-1`\n- [ ] `NightlyReadiness` produces a non-placeholder `.claude/state/harness-readiness-licensed-win-1.yaml`\n- [ ] `MemoryBridgeSync` runs on Windows Git Bash and updates repo-tracked memory outputs without breaking Linux behavior\n- [ ] Any remaining Windows-specific failures are documented with exact repro steps\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2229","display_title":"feat(windows-parity): validate licensed-win-1 NightlyReadiness and MemoryBridgeSync live"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2152","title":"test(reporting): add golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage","issue_number":2152,"body":"## Summary\nAdd the golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage.\n\n## Why\nValidator and renderer work need stable example artifacts representing healthy, degraded, and invalid runs. Fixtures also flush out schema ambiguities early.\n\n## Scope\n- add valid weekly-review run artifact fixtures for minimal, full, degraded, and multi-machine runs\n- add invalid fixtures for missing keys, bad enums, bad timestamps, and malformed sections\n- define expected validator outcomes for invalid cases\n- document fixture naming and refresh rules\n- wire fixtures into validator CI tests\n\n## Deliverables\n- valid and invalid fixture corpus\n- expected validator outputs\n- fixture README\n- CI test usage\n\n## Parent\n- Parent issues: #2146, #2147, #2089\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2152","display_title":"test(reporting): add golden fixture corpus for weekly review run artifacts and validator coverage"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2124","title":"feat(llm-wiki): extend ingestion to Orcina resources, examples, and training materials","issue_number":2124,"body":"## Summary\nExtend llm-wiki coverage to remaining Orcina web content beyond the help system.\n\n## Sources not yet ingested\n1. **Resources page** — https://www.orcina.com/resources/ (videos, webinars, case studies)\n2. **Examples descriptions** — each of the 54 OrcaFlex examples has an online description\n3. **Python training ZIP** — https://www.orcina.com/wp-content/uploads/training/An%20introduction%20to%20the%20Python%20interface%20to%20OrcaFlex.zip\n4. **Release notes** — version-by-version changelog (behavioral changes, new features)\n5. **Blog/news posts** — https://www.orcina.com/news/ (application insights)\n\n## Context\n- Parent: #2088 (core help ingested — 717 topics)\n- Papers already done: 20 PDFs from papers-and-technical-notes page\n- Existing script: `scripts/data/llm-wiki/ingest-orcina.py`\n\n## Value\nRelease notes are especially valuable — they document behavioral changes that affect model compatibility across versions. Training materials fill the gap between reference docs and practical usage.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2124","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): extend ingestion to Orcina resources, examples, and training materials"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2125","title":"feat(llm-wiki): auto-refresh ingestion on new Orcina releases","issue_number":2125,"body":"## Summary\nAdd version detection and incremental refresh to the llm-wiki ingestion pipeline so new OrcaFlex releases are automatically captured.\n\n## Current state\n- ingest-orcina.py does a full crawl every run (~4 min for 717 pages)\n- No version tracking or diff detection\n- Current version: OrcaFlex 11.6c\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Version detection: parse release page to detect new versions\n2. Incremental mode: only fetch pages that changed (use HTTP Last-Modified or content hash)\n3. Changelog generation: diff new vs old index to show what changed\n4. Optional: schedule via cron or GSD trigger on version bump\n\n## Acceptance\n- `ingest-orcina.py --refresh` only downloads changed pages\n- Version stored in index.json metadata\n- Diff report written to data/llm-wiki/changelog/","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2125","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): auto-refresh ingestion on new Orcina releases"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking","issue_number":2112,"body":"## Summary\nBackfill the minimum SubseaIQ equipment-count fields required to unblock #2055 subsea cost benchmarking.\n\n## Context\nAssessment of #2055 found two active blockers:\n1. `status:needs-data`\n2. the required equipment-count fields are absent from every current GoM record in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n\nCurrent state from assessment:\n- required threshold: >=10 GoM fields with equipment counts\n- current state: 0/10 records have `num_trees`, `num_manifolds`, or `tieback_distance_km`\n\nWithout this backfill, the cost benchmarking implementation issue cannot proceed honestly.\n\n## Scope\n- Backfill or derive, for at least 10 GoM fields, the following fields in the SubseaIQ-derived dataset:\n  - `num_trees`\n  - `num_manifolds`\n  - `tieback_distance_km`\n- Document provenance / confidence for each backfilled field\n- Update the dataset and any ingestion/normalization helpers needed to preserve the fields\n- Add a minimal validation check proving the threshold is satisfied\n\n## Target data\n- `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n- any normalization or ingestion surfaces that write/read these fields\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- At least 10 GoM records contain non-null equipment counts and tieback distance\n- Provenance/confidence is documented\n- A validation command or test confirms the threshold is met\n- #2055 can remove `status:needs-data` after verification\n\n## Relationship\n- Direct prerequisite for #2055\n- Should be completed before any cost-per-tree / cost-per-manifold implementation work\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","display_title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055","title":"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts","issue_number":2055,"body":"## Summary\n\nBuild cost-per-equipment-unit benchmark curves (cost/tree, cost/km-flowline, cost/manifold) by water-depth band, correlating SubseaIQ equipment counts against sanctioned project costs.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe scaffold (#1861) provides `SubseaProject` with equipment-count fields and `load_projects()`, but zero cost functions exist. No module currently correlates equipment counts with project costs.\n\n## Data Prerequisites (must be met before implementation)\n\n- [ ] Equipment counts (num_trees, num_manifolds, tieback_distance_km) backfilled for >= 10 GoM fields in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n- [ ] Confirmed name-match mapping between SubseaIQ project names and CostDataPoint project names for overlapping records\n\n## v1 Scope (band-level aggregation)\n\nUse depth-band-level aggregation to compute cost-per-equipment ratios. This avoids per-project name-match fragility and works with sparse data.\n\n### New functions in `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/field_development/benchmarks.py`:\n- `cost_per_tree(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `cost_per_km_flowline(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `cost_per_manifold(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `unit_cost_curves(projects) -> dict` combining all three metrics\n- `cost_benchmark_bands(projects) -> dict` with low/base/high by depth band and concept type\n\n### New helper in `worldenergydata/subseaiq/analytics/cost_correlation.py`:\n- `correlate_equipment_costs(subseaiq_records, cost_records)` — depth-band-level join\n- `aggregate_costs_by_depth_band(merged)` — band-level cost aggregation\n\n## Deferred to v2\n\n- Per-project cost-per-tree correlation (requires CostDataPoint schema extension with equipment fields)\n- Cross-validation of equipment-derived costs against individual sanctioned project costs\n- Full 71-record equipment-count backfill\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Equipment counts populated for >= 10 GoM fields\n- [ ] `cost_per_tree` returns values in $10M-$200M range per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_per_km_flowline` returns values in $5M-$50M range per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_per_manifold` returns physically reasonable values per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_benchmark_bands` returns nested dict with low/base/high structure\n- [ ] All 27 existing `test_benchmarks.py` tests pass unchanged\n- [ ] New cost-related tests added and passing\n- [ ] `benchmarks` exported from `digitalmodel.field_development.__init__`\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- #1861 (scaffold) — DONE\n- Equipment-count data backfill — REQUIRED before implementation\n\n## Labels\n\n`enhancement`, `priority:high`, `cat:engineering`, `dark-intelligence`, `agent:claude`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055","display_title":"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2046","title":"Audit compliance of strict issue planning workflow after rollout","issue_number":2046,"body":"After the new strict planning workflow has been used for a short period, audit compliance across agent activity.\n\nAudit focus:\n- Was `issue-planning-mode` used for all issues?\n- Were plans created in `docs/plans/` using the template?\n- Were adversarial reviews completed before user review?\n- Were labels `status:plan-review` and `status:plan-approved` used correctly?\n- Did any agent begin coding before approval?\n\nDeliverables:\n- Markdown report under `docs/reports/`\n- Compliance summary with examples\n- Gaps, failure modes, and recommendations\n- Decision: keep current approach or escalate enforcement\n\nSuggested trigger:\n- Run after 1-2 weeks of usage or after at least 10 issues have gone through the new workflow\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2046","display_title":"Audit compliance of strict issue planning workflow after rollout"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962","title":"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring — audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode","issue_number":1962,"body":"## Summary\n\nSystematic refactoring of the workspace-hub repo ecosystem, starting with tier-1 repositories. The goal is to reduce architectural debt, consolidate duplicate patterns, enforce consistent structure, and improve agent navigability across all tier-1 repos.\n\n## Tier-1 Repos (Priority Order)\n\n| # | Repo | Purpose | Maturity | Depends On |\n|---|------|---------|----------|------------|\n| 1 | `digitalmodel/` | Engineering digital twin (asset integrity, CP, GIS, hydro) | beta | assetutilities |\n| 2 | `assetutilities/` | Shared engineering utilities (config, units, calculations) | stable | — |\n| 3 | `assethold/` | Asset portfolio financial analysis | beta | assetutilities |\n| 4 | `worldenergydata/` | Global energy market data (BSEE, EIA, drilling) | beta | assetutilities |\n| 5 | `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS/` | CAD solvers, models, database, utilities | beta | — |\n| 6 | `aceengineer-website/` | Consulting website | — | — |\n| 7 | `aceengineer-strategy/` | GTM, business strategy | — | — |\n| 8 | `sabithaandkrishnaestates/` | SKEstates Inc. business ops | — | — |\n\n## Refactoring Scope\n\n### Phase 1: Audit (analysis-only, no code changes)\n- [ ] Run `repo-architecture-analysis` on each tier-1 repo\n- [ ] Identify cross-repo duplicate code (especially assetutilities consumers)\n- [ ] Map dependency graph (imports, shared configs, data flows)\n- [ ] Catalog test coverage per repo (current state)\n- [ ] Identify orphaned/dead code, unused modules, oversized files (>400 lines)\n- [ ] Score each repo against the Control-Plane Contract (AGENTS.md, provider adapters)\n- [ ] Identify skill duplicates across repos (134 known from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 audit)\n\n### Phase 2: Plan (per-repo refactor plans)\n- [ ] Generate refactor plan per tier-1 repo with estimated effort\n- [ ] Prioritize by: business value × technical debt severity × dependency impact\n- [ ] Identify breaking changes and migration paths\n- [ ] Define test coverage targets (tier-1 = 85% per pytest.tier1.ini)\n\n### Phase 3: Execute (TDD, incremental, per-repo)\n- [ ] Execute refactors repo-by-repo following dependency order (assetutilities first)\n- [ ] TDD mandatory — tests before implementation\n- [ ] Each repo refactor as a separate sub-issue for tracking\n\n## Execution Strategy: Claude Code Plan Mode (Cloud)\n\n### Claude Code `--permission-mode plan` (\"Ultraplan\")\n\nClaude Code v2.1.92 supports `--permission-mode plan` which restricts the agent to **read-only analysis and planning** — no file writes, no shell mutations. This is ideal for Phase 1 (Audit) and Phase 2 (Plan).\n\n**Proposed execution:**\n\n```bash\n# Phase 1: Audit each tier-1 repo in plan mode (safe, read-only)\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\n  --print \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\n  -p \"Audit the repository architecture of digitalmodel/. Analyze: module structure, dependency graph, test coverage, dead code, file sizes >400 lines, AGENTS.md compliance. Output a structured JSON report.\"\n\n# Phase 2: Generate refactor plan\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\n  --print \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\n  -p \"Given the audit report, generate a prioritized refactor plan for digitalmodel/. Include: breaking changes, migration steps, test targets, estimated effort per module.\"\n```\n\n### Cloud Execution Considerations\n\n| Aspect | Status | Notes |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| **Claude Code CLI** | Installed (v2.1.92) | Available on ace-linux-1 |\n| **Plan mode** | Supported | `--permission-mode plan` — read-only, safe for audit |\n| **API-key mode** | Supported | `--bare` + `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for headless/cloud runs |\n| **Print mode** | Supported | `-p` / `--print` for non-interactive batch execution |\n| **Budget cap** | Supported | `--max-budget-usd` to control spend per run |\n| **Cloud sandbox** | Possible | Can run via Hermes `delegate_task` with `acp_command='claude'` |\n| **Max subscription** | Claude Max $200/mo | Rate-limited but sufficient for plan-mode audit |\n\n### Recommended Approach\n1. **Phase 1 + 2 (Audit + Plan)**: Run Claude Code in plan mode via `delegate_task(acp_command='claude')` — subagents get isolated context, read-only, budget-capped\n2. **Phase 3 (Execute)**: Requires interactive Claude Code sessions with full permissions — NOT suited for autonomous cloud execution without user oversight\n\n## Adversarial Review Requirements\n\nPer [AI Review Routing Policy](docs/standards/AI_REVIEW_ROUTING_POLICY.md):\n\n- **3-agent adversarial review at BOTH stages** (plan + code/artifact)\n- Claude: orchestrator + reviewer\n- Codex: implementation worker + adversarial reviewer\n- Gemini: architecture reviewer + adversarial reviewer\n- Max 3 review iterations per deliverable\n- All MAJOR findings must be resolved before completion\n\n### Review Checkpoints\n1. **After Phase 1 (Audit)**: All 3 agents review the audit findings\n2. **After Phase 2 (Plan)**: All 3 agents review each repo's refactor plan\n3. **After Phase 3 (Execute)**: All 3 agents review code changes per repo\n\n## Related Issues\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1567 — Feature: Continuous Repo Architecture Intelligence\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1545 — Agentic feature progression for repo ecosystem productivity under GSD\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1603 — Multi-repo architecture scan — expand scanner to all tier-1 repos\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 — Cross-agent session corpus audit (134 duplicate skills identified)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1734 — chore: add domain skills to assethold (tier-1 with 0 skills)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1661 — Architecture scanner: dependency cycle detection and layering violations\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#113 — ROADMAP: Repo ecosystem 3-6 month horizon\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Audit report generated for each tier-1 repo\n- [ ] Refactor plan per repo reviewed and approved by all 3 agents\n- [ ] Phase 3 sub-issues created with clear scope per repo\n- [ ] Test coverage meets tier-1 target (85%) after refactoring\n- [ ] Zero cross-repo duplicate skills remaining\n- [ ] All repos score 100% on Control-Plane Contract compliance\n- [ ] Dependency graph documented and validated\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962","display_title":"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring — audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1782","title":"epic: zero-loss agent learnings — git-track ALL AI agent memories, corrections, patterns, and insights","issue_number":1782,"body":"## Context\n\nA full audit of all 4 AI agents (Claude Code, Hermes, Codex, Gemini) revealed that **~6.7GB of machine-local state** has NO git protection. If ace-linux-1 dies, the entire institutional memory built over months of agent interaction is lost.\n\nThe learnings that matter most — user corrections, learned patterns, permission rules, cross-agent insights — total only ~400KB but are scattered across 4 different home directories with no unified backup or portability strategy.\n\nThis is the umbrella issue coordinating the fix across all agents.\n\n## Architecture: Three Tiers of Portability\n\n### Tier 1: Git-committed snapshots (survives `git clone`)\nSmall, high-value files snapshotted nightly into workspace-hub repo:\n- Agent memory files (Hermes MEMORY.md/USER.md, Claude project memory, Codex rules)\n- Distilled learning artifacts (patterns, corrections, insights, drift summaries)\n- Exported session metadata (JSONL tool-call summaries, not full transcripts)\n\n### Tier 2: Rsync backup (survives single-machine failure)\nLarge files backed up to ace-linux-2 daily:\n- Full session transcripts (Claude 2.5GB, Codex 899MB, Gemini 162MB, Hermes 124MB)\n- SQLite databases (Hermes state.db 53MB, Codex state_5.sqlite 59MB)\n\n### Tier 3: Regenerable (no backup needed)\nCaches, plugins, debug logs, telemetry — recreated on agent install.\n\n## Child Issues\n\n| # | Issue | Agent | What's tracked | Size | Status |\n|---|-------|-------|---------------|------|--------|\n| #1777 | Hermes memory snapshots | Hermes | MEMORY.md, USER.md | 3KB | NEW |\n| #1778 | Un-gitignore hermes/cross-agent state | Hermes/Cross | hermes-insights.yaml, cross-agent-memory.yaml, session JSONL | 15KB+11MB | NEW |\n| #1779 | Claude project memory snapshots | Claude | 39 feedback/project/reference memory files | 196KB | NEW |\n| #1780 | Fix nightly pipeline git-add gap | Claude/All | corrections/, patterns/, cc-insights/, reflect-history/, learned-patterns.json, etc. | ~25MB | NEW |\n| #1781 | Codex + Gemini state snapshots | Codex/Gemini | default.rules, history.jsonl, state.json, session export | ~350KB | NEW |\n| #1583 | Hermes config parity | Hermes | config.yaml template, SOUL.md | existing | OPEN |\n| #894 | Knowledge persistence architecture | All | WRK completion learnings, career knowledge | existing | OPEN |\n| #194 | Codex session archive integration | Codex | Session JSONL export pipeline | existing | OPEN |\n| #193 | Session scanner for historical learnings | Claude | Retroactive learning extraction | existing | OPEN |\n\n## Implementation Order\n\n1. **#1780** (fix nightly pipeline) — Lowest effort, highest impact. Just add `git add` + commit for files already allowed by .gitignore exceptions. ~25MB of corrections, patterns, insights immediately saved.\n\n2. **#1778** (un-gitignore hermes state) — Add 7 gitignore exceptions + commit existing files. Hermes insights, cross-agent memory, drift summary, portfolio signals.\n\n3. **#1779** (Claude project memory) — Snapshot 39 memory files (196KB). Most valuable per-byte: every `feedback_*.md` is a user correction.\n\n4. **#1777** (Hermes memory) — Snapshot MEMORY.md + USER.md (3KB).\n\n5. **#1781** (Codex + Gemini) — Snapshot rules, state, history. Build Codex session export pipeline.\n\n6. **#1583** (config parity) — Already scoped, extends to ace-linux-2.\n\n## Nightly Pipeline Changes (consolidated)\n\nAll snapshot + commit logic should be consolidated into ONE script called at the end of `comprehensive-learning-nightly.sh`:\n\n```bash\nscripts/cron/commit-learning-artifacts.sh\n```\n\nThis script:\n1. Snapshots agent memories (Hermes, Claude, Codex, Gemini) to `config/agents/*/`\n2. `git add` all learning state directories\n3. Runs `legal-sanity-scan.sh --diff-only` (MANDATORY before commit)\n4. Commits and pushes if anything changed\n\n## Legal Risk\n\nMemory files and correction records can contain file paths, command strings, or project names that reference client work. ALL snapshot directories must be covered by `legal-sanity-scan.sh`. The commit script must fail-safe: if legal scan finds violations, skip the commit and log the issue.\n\n## What a Fresh `git clone` Gets You After This\n\n```\nconfig/agents/\n  hermes/memories/          # Hermes MEMORY.md + USER.md snapshots\n  claude/memory-snapshots/  # 39 Claude project memory files\n  codex/state-snapshots/    # default.rules + history.jsonl\n  gemini/state-snapshots/   # state.json + projects.json\n\n.claude/state/\n  corrections/              # Error correction history\n  patterns/                 # Learned behavioral patterns\n  reflect-history/          # Self-reflection analysis\n  cc-insights/              # Agent insights\n  learned-patterns.json     # Distilled pattern database\n  cc-user-insights.yaml     # User preference model\n  hermes-insights.yaml      # Cross-agent Hermes export\n  cross-agent-memory.yaml   # Unified memory index\n  drift-summary.yaml        # Config/skill drift tracking\n  portfolio-signals.yaml    # Repo health signals\n  skill-eval-results/       # Skill evaluation scores\n\nlogs/orchestrator/hermes/   # Hermes session exports (JSONL)\nlogs/orchestrator/codex/    # Codex session exports (JSONL, future)\n```\n\nTotal: ~40MB of institutional memory that survives machine loss.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] All child issues closed\n- [ ] `git clone` on a fresh machine + `sync-agent-configs.sh` restores all agent memories\n- [ ] Nightly pipeline commits all learning artifacts without manual intervention\n- [ ] Legal scan gates all committed learning data\n- [ ] Session transcript rsync backup covers all 4 agents\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1782","display_title":"epic: zero-loss agent learnings — git-track ALL AI agent memories, corrections, patterns, and insights"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583","title":"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates","issue_number":1583,"body":"## Context\nClaude, Codex, and Gemini agent configs are managed via repo templates in `config/agents/{claude,codex,gemini}/` and synced to home directories by `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`. Hermes is not yet covered.\n\nThe `harness-update.sh` cron script already has `update_hermes()` but only runs on ace-linux-1. ace-linux-2 needs inclusion.\n\n## What Needs to Happen\n\n### A) Add Hermes templates to repo\n- `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` — model defaults, toolsets, terminal (no secrets)\n- `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` — persona template\n\n### B) Extend sync-agent-configs.sh\nAdd Hermes to `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`:\n- Template: `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template`\n- Target: `~/.hermes/config.yaml`\n- Merge strategy: template provides defaults, machine-local overrides preserved\n- Skip auth.json and .env (secrets, never in repo)\n\n### C) Custom skills versioning\n- Bundled skills managed by `hermes update` (no action needed)\n- Custom workflow skills → `config/agents/hermes/custom-skills/` in repo\n- Sync script copies/symlinks to `~/.hermes/skills/`\n\n### D) Add ace-linux-2 to harness-update cron\nIn `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml`:\n```yaml\n# Change from:\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1]\n# To:\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1, dev-secondary, ace-linux-2]\n```\nThen run `setup-cron.sh` on ace-linux-2.\n\n### E) Memories policy\n- `MEMORY.md` and `USER.md` are personal/accumulated — do NOT sync via repo\n- They build naturally per machine as Hermes is used\n- Existing `claude-memory-backup` cron already backs up to ace-linux-2\n\n## Files to Create/Modify\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` (new)\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` (new)\n- [ ] `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh` (extend)\n- [ ] `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` (add ace-linux-2 to harness-update)\n- [ ] `docs/ops/scheduled-tasks.md` (update documentation)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Hermes config template exists in repo\n- [ ] sync-agent-configs.sh handles Hermes merge\n- [ ] harness-update cron runs on ace-linux-2\n- [ ] `bash scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh --dry-run` shows Hermes sync\n- [ ] Running sync on a new machine bootstraps Hermes config correctly\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583","display_title":"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates"}]}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"codex_apps","tool":"github_search_issues","arguments":{"repo":"vamseeachanta/workspace-hub","query":"is:issue is:open label:\"status:plan-approved\" created:2026-04-21..2026-04-30","topn":100,"sort":"created","order":"desc"},"result":null,"error":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"codex_apps","tool":"github_search_issues","arguments":{"repo":"vamseeachanta/workspace-hub","query":"is:issue is:open label:\"status:plan-approved\" created:2026-04-21..2026-04-30","topn":100,"sort":"created","order":"desc"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"issues\": [{\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563\", \"title\": \"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control\", \"issue_number\": 2563, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nSet up Telegram-based mobile access to Hermes/AI so Vamsee can interact with the agent, approve/adjust plans, tweak task lists, and monitor/steer work while on the move.\\n\\n## Current user state\\n- Vamsee has logged into Telegram on the mobile device using phone/name.\\n- Next user-side action is to create a Telegram bot via `@BotFather`; the bot token must be handled as a secret and must not be pasted into public issue comments or chat logs.\\n\\n## Recommended architecture\\nUse a Telegram **bot** connected to the Hermes gateway on `ace-linux-1`, not a separate human Telegram account for the AI.\\n\\nRationale:\\n- Telegram bots are the normal integration surface for programmatic message handling.\\n- Bot token can be stored in `~/.hermes/.env` and loaded by the gateway service.\\n- Access can be restricted to Vamsee's user/chat ID.\\n- Hermes gateway already supports Telegram and can run as a persistent service.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### Phase 1 \\u2014 Private single-user Telegram bot\\n- Create bot using `@BotFather`.\\n- Securely store bot token in `~/.hermes/.env` or password manager.\\n- Configure Hermes gateway Telegram integration.\\n- Restrict access to Vamsee's Telegram user/chat ID.\\n- Start gateway as persistent service on `ace-linux-1`.\\n- Verify mobile command/response loop.\\n\\n### Phase 2 \\u2014 Mobile operating guardrails\\n- Keep approval mode `manual` or `smart`; do not use approval `off` for mobile operation.\\n- Enable only mobile-appropriate toolsets initially.\\n- Confirm secrets are never transmitted through Telegram.\\n- Document restart/stop/status commands.\\n- Create quick-reference command sheet for on-the-move tweaks.\\n\\n### Phase 3 \\u2014 Optional enhancements after stable\\n- Voice-message STT/TTS for hands-free operation.\\n- Dedicated group/topic if DMs become cluttered.\\n- Additional user allowlist if needed.\\n\\n## Task list\\n- [ ] Create Telegram bot with `@BotFather` from Vamsee's phone.\\n- [ ] Capture bot username and token securely; do not paste token into GitHub or chat.\\n- [ ] Configure Hermes gateway Telegram platform with `hermes gateway setup` or config edit.\\n- [ ] Add Telegram token to `~/.hermes/.env` and verify service can read it.\\n- [ ] Configure allowed users/chats to Vamsee only for initial rollout.\\n- [ ] Enable appropriate toolsets for mobile use: memory, session_search, cronjob, send_message, file, terminal, browser/web only as needed.\\n- [ ] Install/start gateway persistently: `sudo $(which hermes) gateway install --system`, then start/status.\\n- [ ] If systemd cannot see env vars, add `EnvironmentFile=/home/vamsee/.hermes/.env` to the service and restart.\\n- [ ] Test `/help`, `/status`, a simple task tweak, and a safe approval-gated action from mobile.\\n- [ ] Write a concise mobile command sheet for status checks, plan approvals, task-list updates, pause/kill commands, and GitHub-link requests.\\n- [ ] Optional: configure/test STT/TTS with one short voice note.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- Telegram bot responds to Vamsee from mobile through Hermes gateway.\\n- Unauthorized users/chats are blocked or ignored.\\n- Gateway survives shell logout/reboot via systemd or documented equivalent.\\n- Bot token is stored only in secret/config surfaces, not committed or posted.\\n- Mobile workflow supports: ask status, tweak a task list, request a GitHub link, and approve/revise/hold a plan.\\n- Quick-reference command sheet exists in a durable repo/docs location.\\n\\n## Security / operations notes\\n- Do not paste the bot token into this issue.\\n- Prefer single-user allowlist for the first rollout.\\n- Keep mobile destructive actions approval-gated.\\n- Avoid approval mode `off` unless explicitly approved for a bounded test.\\n- Check gateway logs at `~/.hermes/logs/gateway.log` for troubleshooting.\\n\\n## Suggested labels\\n`enhancement`, `cat:ai-orchestration`, `cat:operations`, `domain:integrations`, `domain:notification`, `priority:high`, `status:pending`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563\", \"display_title\": \"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552\", \"title\": \"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook\", \"issue_number\": 2552, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate a concise policy/runbook for handling non-collaborator comments, unsolicited paid-help offers, and external contributor requests on public repos.\\n\\n## Why\\nIssue #2546 was triggered by an outside commenter offering paid implementation help on #2401. The technical lockdown is now in place for public repos, but operators still need a repeatable response path for existing comments, future exceptions, legitimate contributors, and paid external execution requests.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Document when to ignore, hide, report, or reply to external comments.\\n- Define how to route legitimate contributor requests without granting broad access.\\n- Define paid-pilot intake requirements: scope, NDA/confidentiality, repo access level, payment approval, and PR review gate.\\n- Add a short issue-comment template for declining or redirecting unsolicited offers.\\n- Link the policy from the security/governance docs or ops runbook.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [ ] Runbook covers unsolicited paid-help comments, spam, legitimate contributor requests, and collaborator onboarding.\\n- [ ] Runbook preserves public-repo security posture: no collaborator access without explicit owner approval.\\n- [ ] Templates avoid engaging suspicious accounts while keeping legitimate contributors routed through a safe process.\\n- [ ] Documentation references #2546 and #2401 as the triggering example.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Trigger comment context: #2401\\n- Repository interaction lockdown: #2546\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552\", \"display_title\": \"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550\", \"title\": \"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks\", \"issue_number\": 2550, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nMove the public-repo collaborator-only interaction-limit renewal from a Hermes-local cron reminder into workspace-hub's canonical scheduled-task system, with deterministic verification and issue-comment reporting.\\n\\n## Why\\nIssue #2546 applied `collaborators_only` interaction limits across all public `vamseeachanta/*` repos. GitHub requires interaction limits to expire; the longest supported expiry is `six_months`. A Hermes cron job currently renews every 150 days, but this should be made repo-visible and auditable through workspace-hub scheduled-task governance.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Add a deterministic script that enumerates `vamseeachanta` public repos via `gh repo list`.\\n- Re-apply `gh api -X PUT repos/{owner}/{repo}/interaction-limits -f limit=collaborators_only -f expiry=six_months`.\\n- Verify every public repo reports `limit=collaborators_only` and capture `expires_at`.\\n- Post or prepare a verification report referencing #2546.\\n- Register the job in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` with a cadence safely before expiry, e.g. every 150 days / quarterly equivalent supported by the scheduler.\\n- Add tests for dry-run mode and parser/report formatting.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [ ] Renewal logic is in repo-tracked script(s), not only Hermes-local cron state.\\n- [ ] Script has a dry-run mode suitable for CI/offline validation.\\n- [ ] Script fails non-zero when any public repo is not `collaborators_only` after renewal.\\n- [ ] Schedule metadata includes command, log path, required capabilities, and operator description.\\n- [ ] Documentation links back to #2546 and the current GitHub API limitation requiring expiry.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Root hardening: #2546\\n- Hermes-local renewal job created during closeout: `d9b2d1c2270d`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550\", \"display_title\": \"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533\", \"title\": \"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos\", \"issue_number\": 2533, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nReview and revise the mission/objective statements across all active workspace repos so future GitHub issue work can quickly answer:\\n\\n1. what is this repo for?\\n2. what work belongs here?\\n3. what work should route elsewhere?\\n4. is the repo active, support-only, client/archive, or deprecated/no-new-issues?\\n\\nThis is the portfolio-wide complement to the Tier-1 routing/indexing work already tracked under vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\nThe Tier-1 contract now improves code placement and routing, but the portfolio still needs a canonical mission/objective layer across all active repos. Without that layer, agents can still mis-route issues between overlapping repos such as:\\n\\n- `workspace-hub` vs per-repo execution docs\\n- `assetutilities` vs repo-specific utilities\\n- `digitalmodel` vs client/project verticals\\n- `investments` vs `assethold`\\n- `client_projects` vs client-specific repos\\n- active engineering repos vs archives/reference repos\\n\\n## Initial repo scope\\n\\nUse `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and live immediate-child git repos as the starting inventory.\\n\\n### Tier-1\\n\\n- `workspace-hub`\\n- `digitalmodel`\\n- `assetutilities`\\n- `aceengineer-website`\\n\\n### Tier-2\\n\\n- `OGManufacturing`\\n- `acma-projects`\\n- `frontierdeepwater`\\n- `seanation`\\n- `worldenergydata`\\n- `investments`\\n- `rock-oil-field`\\n\\n### Tier-3 / support / archive candidates\\n\\n- `aceengineer-admin`\\n- `aceengineer-strategy`\\n- `achantas-data`\\n- `achantas-media`\\n- `assethold`\\n- `client_projects`\\n- `doris`\\n- `hobbies`\\n- `pdf-large-reader` if present/active\\n- `sabithaandkrishnaestates`\\n- `saipem`\\n- `sd-work`\\n- `teamresumes`\\n- `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS` if confirmed active in this workspace\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n\\n1. Canonical portfolio mission/objective artifact, preferably `docs/REPO_MISSION_PORTFOLIO.md` or an equivalent linked from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md`.\\n2. Per-repo classification:\\n   - active product/library\\n   - client vertical\\n   - business/admin support\\n   - archive/reference\\n   - deprecated / no-new-issues\\n3. Per-repo mission/objective statement grounded in the strongest available source:\\n   - `.agent-os/product/mission.md`\\n   - `README.md`\\n   - `AGENTS.md`\\n   - `docs/README.md`\\n   - existing workspace-hub portfolio docs\\n4. Per-repo routing rule:\\n   - \\\"work belongs here when...\\\"\\n   - \\\"work should route elsewhere when...\\\"\\n5. Conflict/dedupe notes for overlapping repo missions.\\n6. Update links from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and/or `docs/ROUTING_INDEX.md` so the mission artifact is discoverable.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Every active repo in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` has a current mission/objective row.\\n- [ ] Every immediate-child local git repo not listed in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` is either added, explicitly excluded, or marked as inventory drift.\\n- [ ] Each row has a source path proving where the mission came from.\\n- [ ] Each row has an activity/status classification.\\n- [ ] Each row has an issue-routing rule for work that belongs vs work that should route elsewhere.\\n- [ ] Overlapping missions are explicitly called out and resolved or marked for follow-up.\\n- [ ] Tier-1 mission/objective rows are consistent with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460 and its child routing issues (#2461-#2465).\\n- [ ] The artifact is linked from a canonical workspace discovery surface.\\n- [ ] The plan receives adversarial review before implementation.\\n\\n## Suggested execution waves\\n\\n1. Wave 1: Tier-1 mission alignment and consistency with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\\n2. Wave 2: Tier-2 repo mission/objective review.\\n3. Wave 3: Tier-3/support/archive classification and no-new-issues guidance.\\n4. Wave 4: final portfolio table integration, dedupe/conflict notes, and discoverability links.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Parent/umbrella: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1962\\n- Tier-1 structure epic: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2397\\n- LLM-wiki roadmap context: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13\\n- Tier-1 routing contract: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460\\n- Tier-1 child issues: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2461, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2462, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2463, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2464, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2465\\n\\n## Planning requirements\\n\\nFollow the normal issue gate:\\n\\nIssue \\u2192 resource intelligence \\u2192 canonical plan in `docs/plans/` \\u2192 adversarial plan review \\u2192 `status:plan-review` \\u2192 user approval \\u2192 `status:plan-approved` \\u2192 implementation.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533\", \"display_title\": \"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523\", \"title\": \"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker\", \"issue_number\": 2523, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nBuild a reusable workstation preflight checker for Hermes-led cross-machine dispatch. The checker should turn the manual `ace-linux-2` probe from #2519/#2520 into a repeatable command that verifies host reachability, login-shell tool visibility, provider runtime readiness, GitHub auth state, repo cleanliness, and engineering-tool smoke readiness before work is assigned.\\n\\n## Motivation\\n\\n#2519 established that `ace-linux-2` can run Hermes/Codex only through a login shell and that `gh` auth can independently be invalid. This needs to become an automated gate so future workstations are not delegated work based on stale or partial assumptions.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n- Add a script/CLI entrypoint for workstation preflight checks.\\n- Support at least `ace-linux-1` local mode and `ace-linux-2` remote mode.\\n- Use login-shell checks for remote machines where user-level tools live under `~/.local/bin` or `~/.npm-global/bin`.\\n- Check and redact-sensitive-report:\\n  - hostname / reachability / canonical workspace path,\\n  - git repo branch/status/ahead-behind for tier-1 repos,\\n  - `gh auth status` and `gh api user` success/failure without token values,\\n  - Hermes provider/model config without secrets,\\n  - `codex`, `claude`, `gemini` command availability/version,\\n  - selected engineering tool presence/smoke checks.\\n- Emit both JSON and markdown reports under `docs/reports/` or a configured artifacts directory.\\n- Return non-zero only for clearly blocking failures; classify warnings vs blockers.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- A documented command can run the preflight against `ace-linux-2` via:\\n  `ssh ace-linux-2 'bash -lc \\\"<probe>\\\"'` or equivalent.\\n- Output never includes token/auth file contents.\\n- The current `ace-linux-2` `gh` auth failure is represented as a GitHub-mutation blocker, not as a total machine failure.\\n- Tests cover parsing/classification for at least reachable, auth-invalid, tool-missing, and clean-repo cases.\\n- The #2519 handoff/prompts can reference this checker instead of embedding ad hoc shell snippets.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- Parent: #2519\\n- Blocker/auth repair: #2520\\n- Committed baseline report: `docs/reports/2026-04-27-issue-2519-ace-linux-2-readiness-probe.md`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523\", \"display_title\": \"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker\"}]}"}],"structured_content":{"issues":[{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563","title":"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control","issue_number":2563,"body":"## Summary\nSet up Telegram-based mobile access to Hermes/AI so Vamsee can interact with the agent, approve/adjust plans, tweak task lists, and monitor/steer work while on the move.\n\n## Current user state\n- Vamsee has logged into Telegram on the mobile device using phone/name.\n- Next user-side action is to create a Telegram bot via `@BotFather`; the bot token must be handled as a secret and must not be pasted into public issue comments or chat logs.\n\n## Recommended architecture\nUse a Telegram **bot** connected to the Hermes gateway on `ace-linux-1`, not a separate human Telegram account for the AI.\n\nRationale:\n- Telegram bots are the normal integration surface for programmatic message handling.\n- Bot token can be stored in `~/.hermes/.env` and loaded by the gateway service.\n- Access can be restricted to Vamsee's user/chat ID.\n- Hermes gateway already supports Telegram and can run as a persistent service.\n\n## Scope\n\n### Phase 1 — Private single-user Telegram bot\n- Create bot using `@BotFather`.\n- Securely store bot token in `~/.hermes/.env` or password manager.\n- Configure Hermes gateway Telegram integration.\n- Restrict access to Vamsee's Telegram user/chat ID.\n- Start gateway as persistent service on `ace-linux-1`.\n- Verify mobile command/response loop.\n\n### Phase 2 — Mobile operating guardrails\n- Keep approval mode `manual` or `smart`; do not use approval `off` for mobile operation.\n- Enable only mobile-appropriate toolsets initially.\n- Confirm secrets are never transmitted through Telegram.\n- Document restart/stop/status commands.\n- Create quick-reference command sheet for on-the-move tweaks.\n\n### Phase 3 — Optional enhancements after stable\n- Voice-message STT/TTS for hands-free operation.\n- Dedicated group/topic if DMs become cluttered.\n- Additional user allowlist if needed.\n\n## Task list\n- [ ] Create Telegram bot with `@BotFather` from Vamsee's phone.\n- [ ] Capture bot username and token securely; do not paste token into GitHub or chat.\n- [ ] Configure Hermes gateway Telegram platform with `hermes gateway setup` or config edit.\n- [ ] Add Telegram token to `~/.hermes/.env` and verify service can read it.\n- [ ] Configure allowed users/chats to Vamsee only for initial rollout.\n- [ ] Enable appropriate toolsets for mobile use: memory, session_search, cronjob, send_message, file, terminal, browser/web only as needed.\n- [ ] Install/start gateway persistently: `sudo $(which hermes) gateway install --system`, then start/status.\n- [ ] If systemd cannot see env vars, add `EnvironmentFile=/home/vamsee/.hermes/.env` to the service and restart.\n- [ ] Test `/help`, `/status`, a simple task tweak, and a safe approval-gated action from mobile.\n- [ ] Write a concise mobile command sheet for status checks, plan approvals, task-list updates, pause/kill commands, and GitHub-link requests.\n- [ ] Optional: configure/test STT/TTS with one short voice note.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Telegram bot responds to Vamsee from mobile through Hermes gateway.\n- Unauthorized users/chats are blocked or ignored.\n- Gateway survives shell logout/reboot via systemd or documented equivalent.\n- Bot token is stored only in secret/config surfaces, not committed or posted.\n- Mobile workflow supports: ask status, tweak a task list, request a GitHub link, and approve/revise/hold a plan.\n- Quick-reference command sheet exists in a durable repo/docs location.\n\n## Security / operations notes\n- Do not paste the bot token into this issue.\n- Prefer single-user allowlist for the first rollout.\n- Keep mobile destructive actions approval-gated.\n- Avoid approval mode `off` unless explicitly approved for a bounded test.\n- Check gateway logs at `~/.hermes/logs/gateway.log` for troubleshooting.\n\n## Suggested labels\n`enhancement`, `cat:ai-orchestration`, `cat:operations`, `domain:integrations`, `domain:notification`, `priority:high`, `status:pending`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563","display_title":"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552","title":"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook","issue_number":2552,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a concise policy/runbook for handling non-collaborator comments, unsolicited paid-help offers, and external contributor requests on public repos.\n\n## Why\nIssue #2546 was triggered by an outside commenter offering paid implementation help on #2401. The technical lockdown is now in place for public repos, but operators still need a repeatable response path for existing comments, future exceptions, legitimate contributors, and paid external execution requests.\n\n## Scope\n- Document when to ignore, hide, report, or reply to external comments.\n- Define how to route legitimate contributor requests without granting broad access.\n- Define paid-pilot intake requirements: scope, NDA/confidentiality, repo access level, payment approval, and PR review gate.\n- Add a short issue-comment template for declining or redirecting unsolicited offers.\n- Link the policy from the security/governance docs or ops runbook.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Runbook covers unsolicited paid-help comments, spam, legitimate contributor requests, and collaborator onboarding.\n- [ ] Runbook preserves public-repo security posture: no collaborator access without explicit owner approval.\n- [ ] Templates avoid engaging suspicious accounts while keeping legitimate contributors routed through a safe process.\n- [ ] Documentation references #2546 and #2401 as the triggering example.\n\n## Related\n- Trigger comment context: #2401\n- Repository interaction lockdown: #2546\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552","display_title":"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550","title":"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks","issue_number":2550,"body":"## Summary\nMove the public-repo collaborator-only interaction-limit renewal from a Hermes-local cron reminder into workspace-hub's canonical scheduled-task system, with deterministic verification and issue-comment reporting.\n\n## Why\nIssue #2546 applied `collaborators_only` interaction limits across all public `vamseeachanta/*` repos. GitHub requires interaction limits to expire; the longest supported expiry is `six_months`. A Hermes cron job currently renews every 150 days, but this should be made repo-visible and auditable through workspace-hub scheduled-task governance.\n\n## Scope\n- Add a deterministic script that enumerates `vamseeachanta` public repos via `gh repo list`.\n- Re-apply `gh api -X PUT repos/{owner}/{repo}/interaction-limits -f limit=collaborators_only -f expiry=six_months`.\n- Verify every public repo reports `limit=collaborators_only` and capture `expires_at`.\n- Post or prepare a verification report referencing #2546.\n- Register the job in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` with a cadence safely before expiry, e.g. every 150 days / quarterly equivalent supported by the scheduler.\n- Add tests for dry-run mode and parser/report formatting.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Renewal logic is in repo-tracked script(s), not only Hermes-local cron state.\n- [ ] Script has a dry-run mode suitable for CI/offline validation.\n- [ ] Script fails non-zero when any public repo is not `collaborators_only` after renewal.\n- [ ] Schedule metadata includes command, log path, required capabilities, and operator description.\n- [ ] Documentation links back to #2546 and the current GitHub API limitation requiring expiry.\n\n## Related\n- Root hardening: #2546\n- Hermes-local renewal job created during closeout: `d9b2d1c2270d`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550","display_title":"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533","title":"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos","issue_number":2533,"body":"## Summary\n\nReview and revise the mission/objective statements across all active workspace repos so future GitHub issue work can quickly answer:\n\n1. what is this repo for?\n2. what work belongs here?\n3. what work should route elsewhere?\n4. is the repo active, support-only, client/archive, or deprecated/no-new-issues?\n\nThis is the portfolio-wide complement to the Tier-1 routing/indexing work already tracked under vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Tier-1 contract now improves code placement and routing, but the portfolio still needs a canonical mission/objective layer across all active repos. Without that layer, agents can still mis-route issues between overlapping repos such as:\n\n- `workspace-hub` vs per-repo execution docs\n- `assetutilities` vs repo-specific utilities\n- `digitalmodel` vs client/project verticals\n- `investments` vs `assethold`\n- `client_projects` vs client-specific repos\n- active engineering repos vs archives/reference repos\n\n## Initial repo scope\n\nUse `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and live immediate-child git repos as the starting inventory.\n\n### Tier-1\n\n- `workspace-hub`\n- `digitalmodel`\n- `assetutilities`\n- `aceengineer-website`\n\n### Tier-2\n\n- `OGManufacturing`\n- `acma-projects`\n- `frontierdeepwater`\n- `seanation`\n- `worldenergydata`\n- `investments`\n- `rock-oil-field`\n\n### Tier-3 / support / archive candidates\n\n- `aceengineer-admin`\n- `aceengineer-strategy`\n- `achantas-data`\n- `achantas-media`\n- `assethold`\n- `client_projects`\n- `doris`\n- `hobbies`\n- `pdf-large-reader` if present/active\n- `sabithaandkrishnaestates`\n- `saipem`\n- `sd-work`\n- `teamresumes`\n- `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS` if confirmed active in this workspace\n\n## Deliverables\n\n1. Canonical portfolio mission/objective artifact, preferably `docs/REPO_MISSION_PORTFOLIO.md` or an equivalent linked from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md`.\n2. Per-repo classification:\n   - active product/library\n   - client vertical\n   - business/admin support\n   - archive/reference\n   - deprecated / no-new-issues\n3. Per-repo mission/objective statement grounded in the strongest available source:\n   - `.agent-os/product/mission.md`\n   - `README.md`\n   - `AGENTS.md`\n   - `docs/README.md`\n   - existing workspace-hub portfolio docs\n4. Per-repo routing rule:\n   - \"work belongs here when...\"\n   - \"work should route elsewhere when...\"\n5. Conflict/dedupe notes for overlapping repo missions.\n6. Update links from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and/or `docs/ROUTING_INDEX.md` so the mission artifact is discoverable.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every active repo in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` has a current mission/objective row.\n- [ ] Every immediate-child local git repo not listed in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` is either added, explicitly excluded, or marked as inventory drift.\n- [ ] Each row has a source path proving where the mission came from.\n- [ ] Each row has an activity/status classification.\n- [ ] Each row has an issue-routing rule for work that belongs vs work that should route elsewhere.\n- [ ] Overlapping missions are explicitly called out and resolved or marked for follow-up.\n- [ ] Tier-1 mission/objective rows are consistent with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460 and its child routing issues (#2461-#2465).\n- [ ] The artifact is linked from a canonical workspace discovery surface.\n- [ ] The plan receives adversarial review before implementation.\n\n## Suggested execution waves\n\n1. Wave 1: Tier-1 mission alignment and consistency with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\n2. Wave 2: Tier-2 repo mission/objective review.\n3. Wave 3: Tier-3/support/archive classification and no-new-issues guidance.\n4. Wave 4: final portfolio table integration, dedupe/conflict notes, and discoverability links.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent/umbrella: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1962\n- Tier-1 structure epic: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2397\n- LLM-wiki roadmap context: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13\n- Tier-1 routing contract: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460\n- Tier-1 child issues: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2461, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2462, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2463, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2464, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2465\n\n## Planning requirements\n\nFollow the normal issue gate:\n\nIssue → resource intelligence → canonical plan in `docs/plans/` → adversarial plan review → `status:plan-review` → user approval → `status:plan-approved` → implementation.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533","display_title":"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523","title":"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker","issue_number":2523,"body":"## Summary\n\nBuild a reusable workstation preflight checker for Hermes-led cross-machine dispatch. The checker should turn the manual `ace-linux-2` probe from #2519/#2520 into a repeatable command that verifies host reachability, login-shell tool visibility, provider runtime readiness, GitHub auth state, repo cleanliness, and engineering-tool smoke readiness before work is assigned.\n\n## Motivation\n\n#2519 established that `ace-linux-2` can run Hermes/Codex only through a login shell and that `gh` auth can independently be invalid. This needs to become an automated gate so future workstations are not delegated work based on stale or partial assumptions.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add a script/CLI entrypoint for workstation preflight checks.\n- Support at least `ace-linux-1` local mode and `ace-linux-2` remote mode.\n- Use login-shell checks for remote machines where user-level tools live under `~/.local/bin` or `~/.npm-global/bin`.\n- Check and redact-sensitive-report:\n  - hostname / reachability / canonical workspace path,\n  - git repo branch/status/ahead-behind for tier-1 repos,\n  - `gh auth status` and `gh api user` success/failure without token values,\n  - Hermes provider/model config without secrets,\n  - `codex`, `claude`, `gemini` command availability/version,\n  - selected engineering tool presence/smoke checks.\n- Emit both JSON and markdown reports under `docs/reports/` or a configured artifacts directory.\n- Return non-zero only for clearly blocking failures; classify warnings vs blockers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A documented command can run the preflight against `ace-linux-2` via:\n  `ssh ace-linux-2 'bash -lc \"<probe>\"'` or equivalent.\n- Output never includes token/auth file contents.\n- The current `ace-linux-2` `gh` auth failure is represented as a GitHub-mutation blocker, not as a total machine failure.\n- Tests cover parsing/classification for at least reachable, auth-invalid, tool-missing, and clean-repo cases.\n- The #2519 handoff/prompts can reference this checker instead of embedding ad hoc shell snippets.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: #2519\n- Blocker/auth repair: #2520\n- Committed baseline report: `docs/reports/2026-04-27-issue-2519-ace-linux-2-readiness-probe.md`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523","display_title":"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker"}]}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
